en tree clearances are dimensioned on plans. Check: Landscape plan or site plan dimension lines and notes; verify measurements originate from conductor location, not pole centerline. Outside conductors may be offset 4+ feet from pole center. |\n    85→| EL-2.13 | Tree clearances measured from tree canopy instead of from tree trunk (planting location) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when tree clearances are dimensioned on plans. Check: Landscape plan dimension lines and notes; verify measurements terminate at tree trunk symbol or planting location, not projected canopy edge. |\n    86→| EL-2.14 | Trees not shown on landscape plan in relation to overhead electric facilities, preventing clearance verification | Reviewer Convention | Only when overhead electric facilities are present or proposed. Check: Compare landscape plan to utility plan and site plan; verify all overhead conductors and poles shown on landscape plan or cross-referenced with sufficient clarity to verify clearances. |\n    87→| EL-2.15 | Plans propose trees within prohibited clearance zones without documented Austin Energy written approval | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within restricted zones (< 15 ft distribution, < 50 ft transmission, < 25 ft for large trees from distribution, < 10 ft from poles, < 10 ft from pad-mounts, < 5 ft from underground equipment, or within transmission easement). Check: Review plan notes, attached correspondence, or approval letters for explicit written approval from Austin Energy authorizing the variation. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e54b4ec-2280-4055-b825-43898abbf7bc","uuid":"8747bdd0-2ac8-4d34-8e45-96ff7a88ccab","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/2.md","content":"# Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers tree placement requirements near overhead electric distribution and transmission lines. Requirements address species selection (utility-compatible vs. non-compatible), minimum clearance distances based on infrastructure type and tree size, measurement methodology, and plan documentation to verify compliance. These standards ensure trees do not interfere with electric infrastructure operation and maintenance while protecting public safety.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\nAustin Energy's tree placement requirements establish three distance-based restriction zones around overhead electric facilities. These zones are determined by voltage level (distribution vs. transmission), tree characteristics (species, mature height), and proximity to infrastructure.\n\n**Voltage Classification:**\nElectric facilities are classified by voltage level, which determines applicable clearances:\n- **Distribution facilities**: Energized at less than 60kV phase-to-phase (Austin typical voltages: 12.47kV, 34.5kV)\n- **Transmission facilities**: Energized at 60kV or higher (Austin voltages: 69kV, 138kV, 345kV)\n\nMost overhead facilities on typical development sites are distribution unless explicitly labeled as transmission or located within transmission easements. Transmission facilities are physically larger—taller poles, wider structures, greater conductor spacing—and typically have dedicated easements with special plat notes.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **Utility-compatible trees**: Trees marked with \"x\" in the \"UC\" column of ECM Appendix F; generally mature height ≤ 20 feet with some exceptions (e.g., Goldenball Lead-Tree at 25 ft, Texas Mountain Laurel at 25 ft). The UC designation in Appendix F is definitive, not height alone.\n- **Large trees**: Trees listed in ECM Appendix F with mature height ≥ 40 feet (note: this differs from ECM's general definition of \"large tree\" as 30+ feet; the 40-foot threshold applies for electrical utility purposes)\n- **Distribution clearance zones**:\n  - **< 15 lateral feet**: Only utility-compatible trees permitted\n  - **15-25 lateral feet**: Utility-compatible trees OR trees with mature height < 40 feet\n  - **> 25 lateral feet**: All trees permitted\n- **Transmission clearance**: 50 lateral feet minimum from conductor (only utility-compatible trees permitted within this zone)\n- **Additional prohibitions** (all tree types):\n  - Within transmission easement boundaries (categorical prohibition)\n  - Within 10 lateral feet of any electric utility pole\n  - Within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment (or between access door and drivable surface)\n  - Within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment\n\n**Measurement Methodology:**\nAll clearances measured as **lateral** (horizontal) distance from the tree trunk center (not canopy edge) to the **outside conductor** (not pole centerline or structure). Overhead conductors are offset laterally from poles; measuring from pole centerline significantly underestimates actual clearance. Typical cross arms holding conductors are approximately 8 feet wide, so the outermost conductor may extend 4+ feet from pole center.\n\n**Exceptions:**\nVariations to planting distances and species identified as utility-compatible in ECM Appendix F require **explicit written approval from Austin Energy**. Plans proposing trees within prohibited zones must include documented Austin Energy approval or the violation must be flagged.\n\n**Relationship Between UCM and ECM:**\nUCM § 1.10.10.4 (Austin Energy Design Criteria, Rule No. R161-20.15, 11-9-2020) supersedes ECM 2.4.1.D and 2.4.2.C where they conflict. UCM specifies 15 feet from distribution conductors; ECM specifies 10 feet. UCM specifies 50 feet from transmission conductors; ECM specifies 30-40 feet. The more restrictive UCM distances control.\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- **Species misidentification**: Plant schedule lists trees not marked \"UC\" in ECM Appendix F within restricted zones, or fails to verify UC status against Appendix F\n- **Mature height underestimation**: Plant schedule shows mature height < 40 feet for species ECM Appendix F lists at ≥ 40 feet (e.g., Live Oak Plateau shown as 35 ft when Appendix F specifies 40 ft)\n- **Missing Austin Energy approval**: Plans propose trees within prohibited zones without documented written approval from Austin Energy\n- **Incomplete conductor locations**: Site plan shows utility poles but not conductor positions, preventing accurate lateral distance measurement from conductors as required\n- **Transmission easement planting**: Plans show trees (even utility-compatible) within transmission easement boundaries, violating categorical prohibition\n- **Incorrect measurement reference**: Clearances measured from pole centerline or structure instead of from outside conductor\n- **Incorrect tree reference**: Clearances measured from tree canopy edge instead of trunk center (planting location)\n\n**Root Barriers:**\nTrees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment require root barriers: minimum 4 feet deep, positioned 5 feet from equipment.\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM § 1.10.10.4 - Trees in Proximity to Electric Utility Infrastructure: Utility-compatible trees within 15 lateral feet of distribution conductors and 50 lateral feet of transmission conductors; large tree prohibitions within 25 feet (distribution) and 50 feet (transmission); pole, pad-mount, and underground clearances; transmission easement prohibition; root barrier requirements\n- UCM § 1.14.6 - Vegetation (Transmission Requirements): No trees in transmission easements; utility-compatible requirement within 50 feet of transmission facilities\n- ECM 2.4.1.D - Street Yard Trees: Utility-compatible trees near overhead facilities (note: superseded by more restrictive UCM § 1.10.10.4 distances)\n- ECM 2.4.2.C - Trees in Parking Lots: Utility clearance requirements (note: superseded by UCM § 1.10.10.4)\n- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species: Utility-compatible designation (UC column), mature height classifications\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Landscape Plan / Planting Plan** - Tree locations and spacing shown relative to overhead electric facilities\n- **Site Plan** - Overhead electric distribution and transmission line locations with conductor positions\n- **Plant Schedule / Plant Legend** - Tree species identification, mature heights, and utility compatibility status\n- **Utility Plan** - Existing and proposed electric infrastructure including conductor locations and pole positions\n- **Grading Plan** - Transmission easement boundaries if transmission facilities present\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-2.1 | Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when overhead distribution lines are present or proposed. Check: Site plan for distribution facility locations (voltage < 60kV, typically 12.47kV or 34.5kV; labeled as \"distribution\"; or default assumption for typical overhead lines); cross-reference plant schedule species against ECM Appendix F UC column. |\n| EL-2.2 | Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor or equipment | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when overhead transmission lines are present or proposed. Check: Site plan for transmission facility locations (voltage ≥ 60kV such as 69kV, 138kV, 345kV; labeled as \"transmission\"; transmission easement present; or visually larger structures with taller poles and wider conductor spacing); cross-reference plant schedule species against ECM Appendix F UC column. |\n| EL-2.3 | Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when overhead distribution lines are present or proposed. Check: Site plan for distribution facility locations; cross-reference plant schedule species against ECM Appendix F MH column for trees with mature height ≥ 40 feet. |\n| EL-2.4 | Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor or equipment | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when overhead transmission lines are present or proposed. Check: Site plan for transmission facility locations; cross-reference plant schedule species against ECM Appendix F MH column for trees with mature height ≥ 40 feet. |\n| EL-2.5 | Trees (any species) proposed within transmission easement boundaries | UCM § 1.10.10.4, UCM § 1.14.6 | Only when transmission easement is present on site. Check: Grading plan, site plan, or plat for transmission easement boundaries; verify no trees shown within easement on landscape plan. |\n| EL-2.6 | Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead electric utility pole | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when overhead electric poles are present or proposed. Check: Site plan and utility plan for pole locations; landscape plan for tree locations. |\n| EL-2.7 | Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted electric equipment, or between equipment access door and drivable surface | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when pad-mounted electric equipment is present or proposed. Check: Utility plan for pad-mount transformer locations and access door orientation; landscape plan for tree locations. |\n| EL-2.8 | Trees proposed within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when underground electric equipment (other than duct banks/conduits) is present or proposed. Check: Utility plan for underground equipment locations (pull boxes, vaults, buried transformers); landscape plan for tree locations. |\n| EL-2.9 | Trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers (minimum 4 feet deep, positioned 5 feet from equipment) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Check: Utility plan and landscape plan for distances; landscape plan details or notes for root barrier specifications. |\n| EL-2.10 | Plant legend or plant schedule does not identify species of trees proposed near electric facilities | Reviewer Convention | Only when trees are proposed near overhead or underground electric facilities. Check: Plant legend completeness on landscape plan; verify common and/or scientific names provided. |\n| EL-2.11 | Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) not differentiated on plans to allow verification of clearances from overhead electric facilities | Reviewer Convention | Only when large trees are proposed near overhead electric facilities. Check: Landscape plan and plant schedule for large tree designation or mature height notation. |\n| EL-2.12 | Tree clearances measured from utility pole centerline or structure instead of from outer electric conductor | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when tree clearances are dimensioned on plans. Check: Landscape plan or site plan dimension lines and notes; verify measurements originate from conductor location, not pole centerline. Outside conductors may be offset 4+ feet from pole center. |\n| EL-2.13 | Tree clearances measured from tree canopy instead of from tree trunk (planting location) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when tree clearances are dimensioned on plans. Check: Landscape plan dimension lines and notes; verify measurements terminate at tree trunk symbol or planting location, not projected canopy edge. |\n| EL-2.14 | Trees not shown on landscape plan in relation to overhead electric facilities, preventing clearance verification | Reviewer Convention | Only when overhead electric facilities are present or proposed. Check: Compare landscape plan to utility plan and site plan; verify all overhead conductors and poles shown on landscape plan or cross-referenced with sufficient clarity to verify clearances. |\n| EL-2.15 | Plans propose trees within prohibited clearance zones without documented Austin Energy written approval | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within restricted zones (< 15 ft distribution, < 50 ft transmission, < 25 ft for large trees from distribution, < 10 ft from poles, < 10 ft from pad-mounts, < 5 ft from underground equipment, or within transmission easement). Check: Review plan notes, attached correspondence, or approval letters for explicit written approval from Austin Energy authorizing the variation. |","numLines":87,"startLine":1,"totalLines":87}}}
