{"level":30,"time":1776458890816,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01Hy7wRYjPh9YQZvzvhzFjjd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RF2k3AwaUnXM1pmwofEqzq","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__vision","input":{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Please examine this landscape plan and answer the following:\n\n1. Where are the overhead electric lines (OHE) shown on this plan? Describe their routing and locations across the property.\n\n2. Are there any trees (indicated by circles or tree symbols) shown adjacent to or near the overhead electric lines?\n\n3. What is the approximate horizontal distance between the overhead electric lines and the nearest proposed trees?\n\n4. Are there any notes or callouts indicating that trees near overhead utilities must be a certain species (utility-compatible) or must maintain minimum clearance distances?\n\n5. Are the tree species identified anywhere on this sheet or referenced elsewhere?\n\nPlease provide specific observations about tree placement relative to the overhead lines."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1621,"cache_read_input_tokens":57131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1621},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ebd3136-3d68-467e-99f2-1be8af4a9563","uuid":"84107626-811a-4f6a-9d45-b86307dc7f2e"}
{"level":30,"time":1776458890816,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Please examine this landscape plan and answer the following:\n\n1. Where are the overhead electric lines (OHE) shown on this plan? Describe their routing and locations across the property.\n\n2. Are there any trees (indicated by circles or tree symbols) shown adjacent to or near the overhead electric lines?\n\n3. What is the approximate horizontal distance between the overhead electric lines and the nearest proposed trees?\n\n4. Are there any notes or callouts indicating that trees near overhead utilities must be a certain species (utility-compatible) or must maintain minimum clearance distances?\n\n5. Are the tree species identified anywhere on this sheet or referenced elsewhere?\n\nPlease provide specific observations about tree placement relative to the overhead lines.","msg":"Calling vision tool: 1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777 (31)"}
{"level":30,"time":1776458890816,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0133RLd9f52MFc7Np2j4u3oz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Appendix F\" Austin ECM tree species chart UC designation mature height feet\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Significant Tree Identification\",\"url\":\"https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=375574\"},{\"title\":\"Section 3 - Tree and Natural Area Preservation\",\"url\":\"https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=357827\"},{\"title\":\"APPENDIX F | Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX\",\"url\":\"https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual?nodeId=APXFDECATRSP\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix N. CITY OF AUSTIN PREFERRED PLANT LIST, Environmental Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ecm_appn\"},{\"title\":\"Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX - Municode Library\",\"url\":\"https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual\"},{\"title\":\"20241106-005: Austin Tree Regulation Presentation | Environmental Commission\",\"url\":\"https://bandc.crccheck.com/envboard/440150-20241106-005-austin-tree-regulation-presentation/\"},{\"title\":\"Environmental Criteria Manual - Section 3 (Tree and Natural Area Preservation)- 2021 2nd Quarter Rule Changes | SpeakUp Austin!\",\"url\":\"https://speakupaustin.us.engagementhq.com/dsd-environmental-criteria-manual-section-3-trees-2021-2nd-quarter-rule-changes\"},{\"title\":\"§ 3.3.2. General Tree Survey Standards, Appendix 3.3.0. TREE SURVEYS, Section 3. TREE AND NATURAL AREA PRESERVATION, Environmental Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ecm_sects3_3.3.0_sec3.3.2\"},{\"title\":\"Austin Tree Requirements for Residential Subdivision - Smart Surfaces Policy Tracker\",\"url\":\"https://smartsurfacespolicy.org/policies/austin-tree-requirements-for-residential-subdivision/\"},{\"title\":\"Texas Tree Trials Database | UC Davis Arboretum and Public Garden\",\"url\":\"https://arboretum.ucdavis.edu/tree-database\"}]\n\nI found information about Austin's ECM (Environmental Criteria Manual) and its Appendix F. The chart in Appendix F indicates how each species fits in this general classification.\n\nThe search results indicate that Appendix F contains a tree species chart used for Austin's tree preservation and mitigation requirements. Eligible replacement tree species are listed in ECM Appendix F. \n\nHowever, the actual detailed chart with specific tree species designations (including UC designation classifications and mature height measurements in feet) is referenced in the documents but not fully displayed in the search results. To view the complete Appendix F chart with all the specific details about UC designations and mature heights, you would need to access the full Environmental Criteria Manual document directly through the City of Austin's official ECM resources or the Municipal Code Library at library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPBPYGjbZDsqxm3Nv5ubF2","session_id":"f9937603-308a-417f-be5d-ed14424eb758","uuid":"100a314a-e6de-4644-8583-8b12a3f8f34d"}
{"level":30,"time":1776458890816,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TndkaAMgtGc5777kob6R8q","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Austin Energy Design Criteria Manual tree clearance requirements 40 feet large tree\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"City of Austin Electric Utility Austin Energy Design Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://www.austintexas.gov/sites/default/files/files/Design_Criteria_Manual_Draft_2019.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"City of Austin Electric Utility Austin Energy Design Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://austinenergy.com/-/media/project/websites/austinenergy/contractors/designcriteriamanual.pdf?rev=464b8d8d306c4e4c8584e614e1e35635&hash=849CC0AB5E98C55A06C50EDD42B7CA7A\"},{\"title\":\"Design Reference & Process Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://austinenergy.com/contractors/Construction-Renovation/Design-Reference-and-Process-Documentation\"},{\"title\":\"40' POLE\",\"url\":\"https://austinenergy.com/-/media/project/websites/austinenergy/contractors/15-ft-clearance-drawings.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Section 1. AUSTIN ENERGY DESIGN CRITERIA, Utilities Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://www.austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ucm_sects1\"},{\"title\":\"Mini TOC: SECTION 1 | Utilities Criteria Manual | Austin, TX\",\"url\":\"https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/utilities_criteria_manual?nodeId=S1AUENDECR\"},{\"title\":\"R161-19.07 Austin Energy proposed changes to Utilities Criteria Manual\",\"url\":\"https://www.austintexas.gov/sites/default/files/files/R161-19.07_-_Utilities_Criteria_Manual_Edits_-_Section_1.9.0_METERING.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix 1.10.0. CLEARANCE AND SAFETY REQUIREMENTS, Section 1. AUSTIN ENERGY DESIGN CRITERIA, Utilities Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://www.austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ucm_sects1_1.10.0\"},{\"title\":\"0 AUSTIN UTILITIES Electric Service Rules & Regulations Revised: April 2018\",\"url\":\"https://www.austinutilities.com/assetmanager/downloads/documents/pdf/Electric%20Rule%20&%20Reg%20Policy.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"AUSTIN ENERGY’S OVERHEAD ELECTRICAL LINE CLEARANCES — Okkem Design + Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://www.okkemdesign.com/industry-tips/ae-clearances\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about Austin Energy's clearance requirements from the Design Criteria Manual. Here's what I found regarding tree clearance:\n\nAustin Energy prunes to provide ten feet of side clearance and no overhang dependent upon species, with work based on OSHA 1910.269 requiring anyone trimming within ten feet of an energized conductor to be trained in live line trimming procedures.\n\nRegarding the overhead electrical line clearances more broadly, structures must maintain a 7'-6\" horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from both sides of overhead primary, neutral, and secondary conductors; a 15' radius around the neutral line; and a 15' radius around all primary (live) lines.\n\nThe search results also indicate that Section 1.10.0 is a collection of the various clearance requirements and information concerning temporary working clearances from AE facilities during the construction phase and concerning the final permanent clearances of the Customer's facilities from AE overhead, underground, and padmounted facilities.\n\nHowever, I did not find specific information in these results about tree clearance requirements specifically for \"40 feet large trees.\" For more detailed information specific to large trees and 40-foot clearances, you may want to consult directly with Austin Energy or review Section 1.10.0 of the Austin Energy Design Criteria Manual.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPBPYGjbZDsqxm3Nv5ubF2","session_id":"f9937603-308a-417f-be5d-ed14424eb758","uuid":"8a1d06bc-68df-4621-b01d-d43084137dd5"}
{"level":30,"time":1776458890816,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Efq1q8RuHEWkp6dpdmGnkm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"City Austin tree utility compatible list Live Oak Cedar Elm Texas Mountain Laurel Mexican Redbud\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Residential Tree Planting List\",\"url\":\"https://www.austintexas.gov/sites/default/files/files/Planning/City_Arborist/RecommendedTreePlantingList.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Texas AgriLife Extension City of Austin\",\"url\":\"https://www.cedarparktexas.gov/DocumentCenter/View/4611/Landscape-Plants-Guide-PDF\"},{\"title\":\"Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center - The University of Texas at Austin\",\"url\":\"https://www.wildflower.org/expert/show.php?id=7523&frontpage=true\"},{\"title\":\"The Iconic Trees of Austin, Texas: A Closer Look at the Most Popular Varieties | Sid Mourning Tree Service\",\"url\":\"https://sidmourningtreeservice.com/a-comprehensive-tree-safety-checklist-for-your-commercial-property-2/\"},{\"title\":\"The Best Trees for Central Texas - Lisa's Landscape & Design\",\"url\":\"https://lisalapaso.com/2022/05/04/the-best-trees-for-central-texas/\"},{\"title\":\"Complete List of Trees In Texas | Austin Free Fun Guide\",\"url\":\"https://austin.freefunguides.com/texas-trees-guide/\"},{\"title\":\"Austin Tree Planting Guide: Native Species & City Rules\",\"url\":\"https://www.eimtxcorp.com/austin-tree-planting-guide-native-species-city-rules\"},{\"title\":\"The Shaded Retreat: Designing a Lush and Low-Maintenance Shade Garden in Austin | Top Choice Lawn Care\",\"url\":\"https://www.topchoiceaustin.com/the-shaded-retreat-designing-a-lush-and-low-maintenance-shade-garden-in-austin/\"},{\"title\":\"Shade Tree Selections in Austin, TX | Ted’s Trees, Ltd.\",\"url\":\"https://www.tedstrees.com/our-tree-selection\"},{\"title\":\"Top Trees To Plant In Austin - Austin Tree Surgeons\",\"url\":\"https://austintreesurgeons.com/top-trees-to-plant-in-austin/\"}]\n\nI found search results related to trees in Austin, Texas. Here's what I found:\n\nThe search returned information about recommended trees for Austin and the surrounding Central Texas area. The results include several of the trees you mentioned:\n\n**Live Oak**: The Live Oak is an iconic tree that embodies the spirit of Austin, with its sprawling branches and distinctive, gnarled appearance. These trees are well-suited to Austin's climate, offering ample shade and providing a habitat for local wildlife.\n\n**Cedar Elm**: Ulmus crassifolia (Cedar elm) is also popular in the Austin area and develops yellow-orange leaves in autumn. Right after live oaks, the next most common trees in Austin are cedar elms, making them another great option for planting on nearly any property. Because they are native to Austin, these types of elms are tolerant to drought.\n\n**Texas Mountain Laurel**: Smaller trees worth considering include Cercis canadensis var. texensis (Texas redbud), Sophora secundiflora (Texas mountain laurel), Prunus mexicana (Mexican plum), and Chilopsis linearis (Desert willow).\n\n**Mexican Redbud**: The Mexican or Texas Redbud tree is a medium sized tree that can also be an excellent understory tree. Sporting purple/pink flowers ever spring followed by bright green scalloped leaves all summer and yellow fall color.\n\nThe first search result referenced \"the City of Austin's Appendix F\" which appears to be an official city resource for recommended tree planting lists, though the full details weren't available in the search results shown.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPBPYGjbZDsqxm3Nv5ubF2","session_id":"f9937603-308a-417f-be5d-ed14424eb758","uuid":"274550ec-7a43-4691-8610-350144beb3fb"}
