rkspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/02-electric-review.md","content":"# Electric Review\n\n*Lists the preliminary design requirements and links for Austin Energy review comments.*\n\nElectric Review – Rosemary Avila (512) 972-8488 - rosemary.avila@austinenergy.com\n\nSee Bluebeam link below to download AE site comments:\nLink: https://studio.bluebeam.com/share/epcafnizivuefw\nApplicant, for your comment responses use the color BLUE.\n\nSite Plan Preliminary Design Requirements\nAustin Energy Design Criteria Effective December 12th, 2023\n* UCM § 1.3.8 - Customer Electrical Facilities that Cross Property Lines\n* UCM § 1.3.12 - AE Line Extension Policy\n* UCM § 1.3.16- Truck Access to AE Construction and Existing Facility Sites\n* UCM § 1.9.3 - Installation of Metering Equipment\n* UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Lines and Facilities\n* UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances from AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults\n* UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 - Customer Activity in Utility Easements - Landscaping & Trees\n* Appendix F of the Environmental Criteria Manual\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776458901460,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01KLmo75ErJ9BGd6ACuwjqR4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! This electric review section references the exact codes I need:\n- UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 - Customer Activity in Utility Easements - Landscaping & Trees\n- Appendix F of the Environmental Criteria Manual (ECM Appendix F)\n\nAnd it references \"Bluebeam link\" for detailed comments, but I don't have direct access to that external link. However, this confirms the project has been reviewed by Austin Energy and they're looking at tree clearances from overhead lines.\n\nNow let me think about what I can definitively determine:\n\n**What I Know For Certain:**\n1. Overhead electric lines (OHE) are shown on:\n   - Sheet 9 (Site Plan) - along southern boundary\n   - Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) - along southern boundary\n   - No voltage classification stated = presumed DISTRIBUTION (< 60kV)\n   \n2. Utility poles are shown on Sheet 9 at multiple locations along the southern boundary\n\n3. Proposed trees:\n   - Sheet 35 plant list: Cedar Elm (10), Live Oak (2), Texas Mountain Laurel (2), Mexican Redbud (6)\n   - Sheet 31 vision analysis: Mitigation trees positioned 10-15 feet from OHE line\n\n4. Plan documentation issues:\n   - Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) shows OHE lines and trees but NO dimensions or notes about clearances\n   - No reference to utility-compatible species status\n   - No reference to mature heights for clearance verification\n\n**Gaps in Documentation:**\n- No explicit UC (utility-compatible) designation for proposed tree species\n- No mature height specifications for the proposed species\n- No clearance dimensions or notes on landscape or site plans\n- No Austin Energy approval letter for trees in restricted zones (if any)\n- Plant schedule doesn't indicate which trees are near utility lines\n\nNow, let me think about which checklist items I can evaluate:\n\n\nFor EL-2.1, I can't verify utility-compatible status from the available documentation. Cedar Elm and Live Oak typically reach 40-50 feet at maturity, and if they're positioned 10-15 feet from the distribution line, they'd likely exceed the 25-foot clearance requirement for large trees under EL-2.3. The landscape plan lacks explicit mature height specifications and clearance notes, making it impossible to confirm compliance without additional documentation.\n\nRegarding EL-2.6, the site and landscape plans show pole locations and tree positions, with some trees appearing to fall within the 10-foot pole setback zone, though exact distances aren't clearly dimensioned. For EL-2.10, Sheet 35 does identify all species present, so that requirement is satisfied. However, EL-2.11 presents a significant gap—the plans don't differentiate which trees are expected to reach 40+ feet or provide height projections, preventing verification of whether large tree restrictions are being met.\n\nThe landscape plan does display both trees and overhead electric infrastructure together, addressing EL-2.14. But EL-2.15 remains problematic: without documented Austin Energy approval and without clear height designations for proposed trees, I can't confirm whether any plantings violate the 15-foot clearance for standard trees or 25-foot clearance for large trees. The vision analysis already indicated some trees fall within the 10-15 foot range of the OHE line, which suggests potential non-compliance.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":614,"cache_read_input_tokens":73464,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":614},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"s