{"level":30,"time":1776459690486,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CJVBffmCw9WyxmyLajHXQG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Austin ECM Appendix F utility compatible trees UC 40 feet mature height specifications\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Significant Tree Identification\",\"url\":\"https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=375574\"},{\"title\":\"Section 3 - Tree and Natural Area Preservation\",\"url\":\"https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=357827\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix N. CITY OF AUSTIN PREFERRED PLANT LIST, Environmental Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ecm_appn\"},{\"title\":\"Residential Tree Planting List\",\"url\":\"https://www.austintexas.gov/sites/default/files/files/Planning/City_Arborist/RecommendedTreePlantingList.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX - Municode Library\",\"url\":\"https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual\"},{\"title\":\"§ 2.4.1. Street Yard, Appendix 2.4.0. BASIC REQUIREMENTS, Section 2. LANDSCAPE, Environmental Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ecm_sects2_2.4.0_sec2.4.1\"},{\"title\":\"20241106-005: Austin Tree Regulation Presentation | Environmental Commission\",\"url\":\"https://bandc.crccheck.com/envboard/440150-20241106-005-austin-tree-regulation-presentation/\"},{\"title\":\"APPENDIX F | Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX\",\"url\":\"https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual?nodeId=APXFDECATRSP\"},{\"title\":\"§ 3.3.2. General Tree Survey Standards, Appendix 3.3.0. TREE SURVEYS, Section 3. TREE AND NATURAL AREA PRESERVATION, Environmental Criteria Manual, Austin\",\"url\":\"http://austin-tx.elaws.us/code/ecm_sects3_3.3.0_sec3.3.2\"},{\"title\":\"CITY OF AUSTIN – DEVELOPMENT SERVICES DEPARTMENT\",\"url\":\"https://zilkerneighborhood.org/dev/1209AzieMorton/1209AzieMorton-site_plan_MRC0_(2020-03-05).pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the Austin Environmental Criteria Manual (ECM) Appendix F and utility-compatible trees. Here's what I found:\n\nIn areas where utility lines are present or proposed, only trees from the Utility Compatible Shade Trees list (see Appendix F) shall be planted within: a) 10 lateral feet from any overhead distribution conductor; b) 30 to 40 lateral feet from any overhead transmission conductor.\n\nThe search results reference ECM Appendix F as containing specifications for tree species, including the Utility Compatible Shade Trees list and descriptive categories of tree species. However, the specific detailed specifications from Appendix F regarding mature height of 40 feet for UC (utility compatible) trees were not fully displayed in the search results I retrieved.\n\nTo access the complete specifications from Austin ECM Appendix F with the specific tree species listings and their mature height characteristics (including the 40-foot mature height category you're asking about), I recommend:\n\n1. Visiting the City of Austin's official Environmental Criteria Manual documentation directly\n2. Checking the Municode Library version at library.municode.com for Austin's ECM\n3. Contacting the City of Austin's Development Services or City Arborist office\n\nThe search results confirm that Appendix F exists and contains this information, but the detailed tree-by-tree specifications with mature heights were not fully accessible in my search results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPBPYGjbZDsqxm3Nv5ubF2","session_id":"f9937603-308a-417f-be5d-ed14424eb758","uuid":"3d9ed595-c174-4e17-a76c-203fdf39e52c"}
{"level":30,"time":1776459690486,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QxkJy2Dvk5ySn6FSGCdbyB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"City Austin electrical clearance tree species 40 feet large trees 10 lateral feet overhead\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"City Council Greenlights Additional Tree Trimming Funding for Austin Energy\",\"url\":\"https://austinenergy.com/about/news/news-releases/2024/city-council-greenlights-tree-trimming-funding-for-austin-energy\"},{\"title\":\"Tree Trimming\",\"url\":\"https://austinenergy.com/en/residential/residential-services/tree-trimming\"},{\"title\":\"'We'll be working with our policymakers' | Austin power line tree trimming rules may be re-evaluated | kvue.com\",\"url\":\"https://www.kvue.com/article/news/investigations/defenders/austin-power-line-tree-trimming-rules-reevaluated/269-d57e1ba8-ed90-4fbf-aa7a-7aa6d705389a\"},{\"title\":\"FAQs\",\"url\":\"https://austinenergy.com/residential/residential-services/tree-trimming/faqs\"},{\"title\":\"Trees & Power Lines\",\"url\":\"https://cityofredding.gov/government/departments/redding_electric_utility/in_our_community/electric_safety/trees___power_lines.php\"},{\"title\":\"Trees & Powerlines\",\"url\":\"https://www.brightridge.com/resources/trees-and-powerlines/\"},{\"title\":\"City Plants | Yard Tree Species List\",\"url\":\"https://www.cityplants.org/yard-tree-species-list/\"},{\"title\":\"Tree Information Center | AustinTexas.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.austintexas.gov/department/tree-information-center\"},{\"title\":\"AUSTIN\",\"url\":\"https://coautilities.com/wps/wcm/connect/occ/21d8aeaf-0f41-4364-951f-3113bd166fbe/OCT_19_AUNow_ENG.pdf?MOD=AJPERES&CONVERT_TO=url&CACHEID=ROOTWORKSPACE.Z18_G0L8HOC0LGEC40AMK6T8400004-21d8aeaf-0f41-4364-951f-3113bd166fbe-mS5I8YF\"},{\"title\":\"ZNA-Trees\",\"url\":\"https://zilkerneighborhood.org/trees.shtml\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding Austin's electrical clearance requirements for trees:\n\n## Tree Trimming Clearance Requirements\n\nFast-growing trees like pecan have a 15-foot clearance, while slow-growing species like cedar have a 10-foot clearance. Trees near high-voltage transmission cables must be trimmed 25 feet back.\n\n## Tree Size Restrictions\n\nTrees within 20 feet of power lines should be smaller — less than 25 feet high fully grown. For larger trees, those with a mature height of 40+ feet should be planted at least 50 feet from a pole.\n\n## Species Considerations\n\nSlow-growing species such as Cedar, Cedar Elm, and Juniper require 10-foot clearance. The clearance requirements vary based on the growth rate of the species and the voltage of the power lines in question.\n\nThe search results show Austin Energy's standards focus on maintaining adequate clearances based on tree species growth rates and the type of electrical infrastructure, rather than a single standard for all trees of 40+ feet height with 10 lateral feet clearance as specified in your query.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPBPYGjbZDsqxm3Nv5ubF2","session_id":"f9937603-308a-417f-be5d-ed14424eb758","uuid":"f48f1b4e-1a63-4e5a-a539-c6c65f1de146"}
