{"level":30,"time":1776459690486,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPBPYGjbZDsqxm3Nv5ubF2","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my extensive research, I've found that the specific detailed data you're requesting from the City of Austin Environmental Criteria Manual (ECM) Appendix F is publicly referenced but the actual accessible document content with the complete species-by-species utility compatibility designations and mature height specifications is not readily available through web sources.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n### Document References Located\n- **ECM Appendix F** exists and contains a \"Descriptive Categories of Tree Species\" chart used for tree preservation and mitigation requirements in Austin\n- **ECM §2.4.1 and §2.4.2** specify that trees planted within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors (or 30-40 feet from transmission conductors) must come from the \"Utility Compatible Shade Trees\" list in Appendix F\n\n### Species Information Found\n\n**Live Oak (Quercus virginiana)**\n- Mature height: 50 feet (commonly, can spread 100+ feet wide)\n- Status: Iconic Austin tree, native to region\n- Likely qualifies as \"large tree\" (40+ feet minimum threshold)\n\n**Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana)**\n- Mature height: 12-20 feet (typically smaller than other Cercis varieties)\n- Status: Described as more compact in Texas\n- Does NOT qualify as \"large tree\" (under 40-foot threshold)\n\n**Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) & Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora)**\n- Specific mature height data from ECM not found in accessible sources\n- Cedar Elm: Noted as a slow-growing species requiring 10-foot clearance from power lines\n\n### Electrical Clearance Standards Found\n- Slow-growing species (Cedar, Cedar Elm, Juniper): 10-foot clearance\n- Fast-growing species (Pecan): 15-foot clearance\n- High-voltage transmission lines: 25-foot clearance\n- Trees within 20 feet of power lines: Maximum 25 feet mature height recommended\n- Trees 40+ feet mature height: Plant at least 50 feet from poles\n\n### Sources\n- [Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX - Municode Library](https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual)\n- [APPENDIX F | Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX](https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual?nodeId=APXFDECATRSP)\n- [Austin Energy Tree Trimming FAQs](https://austinenergy.com/residential/residential-services/tree-trimming/faqs)\n- [Cercis canadensis var. mexicana - Oregon State University](https://landscapeplants.oregonstate.edu/plants/cercis-canadensis-var-mexicana)\n- [USDA Forest Service - Quercus virginiana](https://www.fs.usda.gov/database/feis/plants/tree/quevir/all.html)\n\n**Note:** To obtain the complete UC designation status and verified mature heights for all four species from the official ECM Appendix F chart, you should contact the City of Austin's Development Services Department or access the full Environmental Criteria Manual document directly through their website."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac1abd048130bc5e1 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 31967\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 557502</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9937603-308a-417f-be5d-ed14424eb758","uuid":"10db14c2-be85-42dc-af81-f9d2f0fb2148","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Research the utility compatibility status and mature heights of these four tree species according to City of Austin Environmental Criteria Manual (ECM) Appendix F:\n\n1. Live Oak (Quercus virginiana)\n2. Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia)\n3. Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora)\n4. Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana)\n\nFor each species, find:\n- Whether it has a \"UC\" (utility-compatible) designation in ECM Appendix F\n- The listed mature height per ECM Appendix F\n- Whether it qualifies as a \"large tree\" (40+ feet) for electrical utility purposes per UCM § 1.10.10.4\n- Any specific electrical clearance restrictions\n\nSearch for the most current version of Austin's ECM Appendix F and ECM 2.4.1 and 2.4.2 which reference utility-compatible trees.","agentId":"ac1abd048130bc5e1","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my extensive research, I've found that the specific detailed data you're requesting from the City of Austin Environmental Criteria Manual (ECM) Appendix F is publicly referenced but the actual accessible document content with the complete species-by-species utility compatibility designations and mature height specifications is not readily available through web sources.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n### Document References Located\n- **ECM Appendix F** exists and contains a \"Descriptive Categories of Tree Species\" chart used for tree preservation and mitigation requirements in Austin\n- **ECM §2.4.1 and §2.4.2** specify that trees planted within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors (or 30-40 feet from transmission conductors) must come from the \"Utility Compatible Shade Trees\" list in Appendix F\n\n### Species Information Found\n\n**Live Oak (Quercus virginiana)**\n- Mature height: 50 feet (commonly, can spread 100+ feet wide)\n- Status: Iconic Austin tree, native to region\n- Likely qualifies as \"large tree\" (40+ feet minimum threshold)\n\n**Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana)**\n- Mature height: 12-20 feet (typically smaller than other Cercis varieties)\n- Status: Described as more compact in Texas\n- Does NOT qualify as \"large tree\" (under 40-foot threshold)\n\n**Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) & Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora)**\n- Specific mature height data from ECM not found in accessible sources\n- Cedar Elm: Noted as a slow-growing species requiring 10-foot clearance from power lines\n\n### Electrical Clearance Standards Found\n- Slow-growing species (Cedar, Cedar Elm, Juniper): 10-foot clearance\n- Fast-growing species (Pecan): 15-foot clearance\n- High-voltage transmission lines: 25-foot clearance\n- Trees within 20 feet of power lines: Maximum 25 feet mature height recommended\n- Trees 40+ feet mature height: Plant at least 50 feet from poles\n\n### Sources\n- [Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX - Municode Library](https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual)\n- [APPENDIX F | Environmental Criteria Manual | Austin, TX](https://library.municode.com/tx/austin/codes/environmental_criteria_manual?nodeId=APXFDECATRSP)\n- [Austin Energy Tree Trimming FAQs](https://austinenergy.com/residential/residential-services/tree-trimming/faqs)\n- [Cercis canadensis var. mexicana - Oregon State University](https://landscapeplants.oregonstate.edu/plants/cercis-canadensis-var-mexicana)\n- [USDA Forest Service - Quercus virginiana](https://www.fs.usda.gov/database/feis/plants/tree/quevir/all.html)\n\n**Note:** To obtain the complete UC designation status and verified mature heights for all four species from the official ECM Appendix F chart, you should contact the City of Austin's Development Services Department or access the full Environmental Criteria Manual document directly through their website."}],"totalDurationMs":557502,"totalTokens":31967,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":782,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3589,"cache_read_input_tokens":26838,"output_tokens":758,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3589},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776459690486,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01HUqWFZK3Jt6HDaiDq3QmDi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The agent research found some useful information but didn't locate the actual ECM Appendix F data definitively. The research indicates:\n\n**Live Oak**: 50-foot mature height - definitely qualifies as a \"large tree\" (40+ ft)\n**Mexican Redbud**: 12-20 feet - does NOT qualify as large tree\n**Cedar Elm**: Limited info, but mentioned as needing 10-foot clearance from power lines\n**Texas Mountain Laurel**: Limited specific info\n\nThe research shows that the documents reference utility-compatible designations in Appendix F, but the actual designation for each species isn't accessible through the web search.\n\nHowever, I have enough information to make determinations:\n\n1. The site has **overhead electric lines running along the southern boundary**\n2. **Six mitigation trees are planted directly beneath/through these overhead lines**\n3. **No utility-compatible designation or clearance information is provided on any of the plans**\n4. **At least one species (Live Oak at 50 feet mature height) qualifies as a \"large tree\" (40+ feet)**\n5. **No Austin Energy written approval is documented on the plans for any of these trees**\n\nNow I need to evaluate each checklist item systematically. Let me check if I can find more specific information about the voltage/type of the overhead electric lines (distribution vs. transmission), and measure the actual distances if possible.\n\nLet me use the measure-distance tool to determine the actual lateral distances between the proposed mitigation trees and the overhead electric line and poles.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_t