ing Wall Wall at Existing Concrete Retaining Wall**\", and \"**Tie to Existing Concrete Retaining Wall**\".\n\n**5. Driveways and parking areas relative to overhead lines:**\n*   The primary internal driveway enters from the west, splits, and forms a loop around the site's interior. \n*   The southern leg of this internal driveway runs east-west, parallel to Bldg. 7, Bldg. 8, the southern retaining wall, and the southern \"— OHE —\" line. \n*   The eastern leg of the driveway runs parallel to Bldg. 5, Bldg. 6, the eastern retaining wall, and the eastern \"— OHE —\" line.\n*   Parking spaces (compact and standard) are positioned along these driveways. Because the OHE lines are located on the extreme outer perimeter of the property boundaries, the driveways and parking areas do not appear to be directly underneath the OHE lines, but they are adjacent to them, separated by the property setbacks, landscape buffer areas, and retaining walls.\n\n**6. Building overhangs, awnings, or canopies extending toward overhead lines:**\n*   The building footprints feature dashed perimeter lines that typically indicate roof overhangs or eaves. \n*   While these dashed lines extend slightly outward from the solid footprint lines of Bldg. 5, 6, 7, and 8, they appear to stay well within the property boundaries and building setback limits. \n*   There is no visual evidence on this plan of any building overhangs, large awnings, or canopies physically extending over the property line or directly crossing into the path of the indicated \"— OHE —\" lines.\n\n**7. Clearance dimensions or notes related to overhead electric facilities:**\n*   A thorough review of the drawing space, dimensional arrows, and the provided text sections (including \"Site Plan Release Notes\", \"Lighting Note\", \"Trash Collection Notes\", \"Accessibility Notes\", \"Legal Description\", and the \"Texas 811 Call Before You Dig!!\" box) yields **no explicit clearance dimensions or specific warning notes** regarding ver