sign (+)\"\n\nThe wavy/radiating lines on Symbol 1 could be describing the Texas Mountain Laurel's scalloped/cloud edge. But the plus sign on Symbol 2 matches Live Oak, not a mitigation/ornamental tree. This is confusing because the south boundary should only have mitigation trees marked with \"M\". The plus sign symbol shouldn't appear there if it's designated for Live Oak in the shade tree category. I need to look more carefully at what the vision actually showed on Sheet 31 to clarify whether these symbols are being used differently than the plant schedule suggests, or if I'm misinterpreting the visual descriptions.\n\nRegardless of the exact species identification, the critical issue is clear: trees are planted at 0 feet lateral distance from the OHE line, which violates tree clearance requirements. Now I'll work through the checklist items to assess compliance, starting with EL-2.1 regarding non-utility-compatible trees within 15 feet of distribution conductors.\n\nTrees at 0 feet fall well within the 15-foot threshold, but the determination hinges on whether these are utility-compatible species. Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud are generally classified as utility-compatible, which would normally allow closer placement. However, a 0-foot distance is problematic regardless of compatibility status—there's no physical space for conductor clearance. The 0-foot measurement likely reflects overlapping plan symbols rather than actual ground conditions, suggesting some separation exists in practice, though the plans don't clearly document it.\n\nFor EL-2.3, I need to evaluate whether any trees exceed 40 feet in height within 25 feet of the distribution conductor. Texas Mountain Laurel typically reaches maximum heights around 25 feet, so this threshold may not be exceeded. boundary trees at the OHE line (EL-2.15).\n\nI should verify the vision analysis about tree count on the south boundary. The initial observation noted three trees marked \"M\" along the southern boundary near the `-OHE-` line, with a fourth tree marked \"H.8\" at the far southeast corner, suggesting the total count may be higher than initially stated.\n\nThe clearest violations appear to be: species aren't labeled on the landscape plan itself (Sheet 31), only on a separate sheet, which violates EL-2.10. Additionally, depending on what species occupy those positions at 0 feet from the OHE line, there could be violations under EL-2.1, EL-2.3, or EL-2.15 regarding setback requirements.\n\nGiven measurement uncertainties and medium-confidence data, I should flag definitive violations while marking ambiguous cases as unverifiable. I also need to check whether the electrical or landscape plans contain any notes explaining these placements or documenting Austin Energy approval. The core problem is that the plant schedule exists only on Sheet 35 (Calculations), separate from Sheet 31 (Landscape) where the trees are actually shown, creating a disconnect in how the plan presents required information.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2593,"cache_read_input_tokens":60820,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2593},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e54b4ec-2280-4055-b825-43898abbf7bc","uuid":"7a4cffef-f8de-4605-bbc2-0c55f3398d29"}
