cumentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Three mitigation trees are planted at 0 feet lateral distance directly under overhead electric conductor, and one at 12.6 feet, all within restricted clearance zones. No documentation of Austin Energy written approval appears on site plan, landscape plan, or supplementary documents reviewed. Variance or exception authorization required per UCM § 1.10.10.4 is not provided."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"34d91d26-f27f-43a5-b8a1-38b9e75edd0e"}
{"level":30,"time":1776460264428,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01BTiQzkRBB4xXDQNcXgS5wN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the general notes section has site plan release notes and fire department notes, but I don't see any note about the 48-hour notification requirement for overhead electric utilities (EL-1.46). Also, no specific clearance dimensions or requirements for overhead lines are mentioned.\n\nNow let me create a comprehensive evaluation. Based on my research, here are the critical findings:\n\n**CRITICAL FINDINGS:**\n\n1. **Overhead Electric Lines Present**: The existing conditions survey (Sheet 4) clearly shows overhead utility lines and poles along Valley View Road. These lines are shown again on the site plan (Sheet 9) and landscape plan (Sheet 31).\n\n2. **Trees Within Clearance Distance**: Sheet 31 shows trees proposed within 5-10 feet of the overhead electric line. According to EL-1.37, trees must be utility-compatible species if within 10 lateral feet of distribution conductors or within 50 lateral feet of transmission conductors.\n\n3. **No Species Verification**: While proposed trees are Live Oak and Cedar Elm, there is NO note on the landscape plan specifying that these must be utility-compatible species or stating clearance distances. I need to verify if these species are utility-compatible per ECM Appendix F.\n\n4. **No Clearance Dimensions Provided**: The site plan (Sheet 9) and electrical plan (Sheet 21) show no clearance dimensions, notes, or exhibits demonstrating that required clearances are maintained from overhead electric facilities to buildings, parking, driveways, etc.\n\n5. **No 48-Hour Notification Note**: The general notes section does not include the required note about notifying Austin Energy 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage lines.\n\n6. **No Building Elevation or Section Exhibits**: There are no building elevation or section views showing vertical clearances from overhead conductors to buildings or other structures.\n\n7. **No Proximity Survey**: The grouping states that a proximity survey is required showing proximities of existing and proposed facilities to existing Austin Energy primary voltage facilities per UCM § 1.10.3.\n\n\nThis site has existing overhead electric facilities along Valley View Road, proposed buildings within the clearance envelope, parking and driveways, and landscaping near the lines, so the grouping clearly applies. Looking at the checklist items, EL-1.1 is relevant since buildings are positioned near overhead conductors and need to maintain the required 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance. The plans don't provide evidence of this clearance being met.\n\nBuildings 1 and 8 sit 26.1' and 33.3' from Valley View Road, but these distances don't confirm clearance from the actual conductor line since the conductor may be offset from the road edge. Without section views or elevation exhibits showing vertical clearances from the outer conductor to building faces, I can't verify compliance. EL-1.2 also applies to the parking and customer facilities proposed on-site, which need to maintain 15-foot radial clearance from distribution conductors. Again, no clearance dimensions are shown for any of these facilities, making verification impossible without additional exhibits showing the required clearances from the outside conductor.\n\nFor EL-1.3, the site plan shows proposed buildings but lacks roofline and overhang details needed to assess whether projections maintain clearances from overhead conductors. Architectural plans with elevation sheets would be required to verify this. Similarly, EL-1.6 addresses buildings near the ROW—while Buildings 1 and 8 are documented at specific distances from the front property line, section views showing the actual relationship between building faces and electric facilities in the ROW are not provided.\n\nEL-1.7 identifies a critical gap: no building elevation sheets were submitted that would show surveyed overhead conductor locations and elevations. This is a required submission element. EL-1.8 compounds this issue—across all submitted sheets, there are no dimension lines or clearance exhibits indicating distances from the outer conductor to building edges, which is necessary to demonstrate compliance with clearance standards.\n\nMoving to driveway clearances, EL-1.18 notes that while driveways are positioned adjacent to overhead lines on the site plan, the grading plan and sections lack vertical clearance dimensions from the overhead facilities to driveway grades. Without this data—particularly mid-span clearance measurements—it's impossible to verify the required 16-18 foot clearances are maintained. EL-1.23 addresses measurement methodology: since no clearance dimensions appear anywhere on the plans, I cannot determine whether measurements are being taken from the outside conductor or from the pole centerline, which would affect compliance assessment.\n\nThe surveyor data requirement under EL-1.31 is a clear failure—no registered surveyor has provided conductor elevations or mid-span clearance measurements despite overhead facilities being present on the site. For EL-1.37, the landscape plan shows trees positioned 5-10 feet from the overhead electric line on the southern boundary, with 2 Live Oak and 10 Cedar Elm trees proposed. The critical gap is that the landscape plan contains no specification requiring these trees to be utility-compatible species or noting the clearance maintenance requirements. proposed Live Oak and Cedar Elm are marked as utility-compatible. Since the landscape plan lacks any notation confirming UC status, doesn't show clearance dimensions, and the standard explicitly requires verification against the Utility Compatible list, this appears to be a violation.\n\nFor EL-1.46, I need to check if the required 48-hour notification note is present. Reviewing the cover sheet and general notes, there's no statement requiring the owner or contractor to notify Austin Energy before work near high-voltage lines as mandated by Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752. This is also a failure.\n\nMoving through the remaining items: gas pump canopies don't apply here. Stairs and elevated walkways near overhead lines aren't shown. Fences require verification—both standard fencing and vehicle barriers need checking against the plans. Retaining walls appear on sheets 38-39, so I need to confirm they maintain proper clearances. Emergency access structures aren't present. Parking areas also need review to ensure compliance.\n\nFor the additional items, streetlight poles under lines aren't applicable since they're not positioned that way. Sidewalks should be verified for adequate pole clearance. Customer facilities, water meters, and water lines all need checking to confirm they're appropriately distanced from electrical infrastructure. Foundations within 5 feet of property lines require verification. Line crossing angles and secondary-over-primary configurations don't apply to existing infrastructure.\n\nMarquee signs aren't present on the plans. Decorative lighting, meter obstruction, development proximity to streetlights, and down guy wire conflicts all need review. Guy easements would only be relevant if transmission or down guy systems are depicted. Pad-mounted transformers shown on the electrical plan need clearance verification. Construction staging areas should be checked for adequate separation from electrical facilities.\n\nService masts through roofs aren't indicated in the civil drawings. I should examine the plans more thoroughly for retaining walls, water infrastructure, parking layout, driveway configuration, and transformer placement to ensure all electrical standards are met.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2261,"cache_read_input_tokens":64465,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2261},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ebd3136-3d68-467e-99f2-1be8af4a9563","uuid":"4dc49f4a-fdc6-4c9b-9a09-9ad8f0c4d13a"}
