{"level":30,"time":1776460355553,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0131AQrUaXL2rsudupV6fBtS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've submitted my findings. Let me now provide a summary of the findings to the user so they understand what I found and what the key issues are.\n\nThe grouping has been evaluated and I found:\n- **1 FAIL item**: EL-1.37 (Trees not utility-compatible for overhead line proximity)\n- **8 NOT-VERIFIABLE items**: EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.7, EL-1.8, EL-1.23, EL-1.31, EL-1.42, EL-1.46\n\nThe key issues are:\n1. No clearance dimensions shown from overhead electric lines to buildings, structures, trees\n2. No building elevation sheets with surveyed conductor locations\n3. Trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) proposed near overhead lines are not utility-compatible species\n4. No 48-hour notification note on plans\n5. No easement documentation for down guy anchor\n\nAll other items (EL-1.2, EL-1.4 through EL-1.6, EL-1.9 through EL-1.22, EL-1.24 through EL-1.30, EL-1.32 through EL-1.41, EL-1.43 through EL-1.45, EL-1.47) were evaluated as PASS or NOT APPLICABLE because:\n- Most items either did not apply to this project (e.g., no gas pump canopies, no transmission lines, no marquee signs, no enclosed trash areas, etc.)\n- Items that did potentially apply had no evidence of the deficiency being present\n\nFor example:\n- EL-1.2 (15-foot radial clearance from distribution conductors): Not marked as fail/not-verifiable because buildings are far enough away\n- EL-1.9 (Fence clearance): Not marked because no fences proposed near overhead lines\n\n\n- EL-1.10 (Fence posts 10 feet from poles): Not marked because no fences proposed\n- EL-1.18 (Driveway clearance): Not marked - driveway grades shown on site plan do not appear to cross directly under overhead lines based on the sheets reviewed\n\nThe review is complete with all applicable standards addressed.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3287,"cache_read_input_tokens":65775,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3287},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1a6fb5a-883d-4cd9-82be-985a56b0d602","uuid":"d3eb9016-2a68-47f8-96f6-1ed474aaeca9"}
{"level":30,"time":1776460355553,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0131AQrUaXL2rsudupV6fBtS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary of Site Plan Review Findings\n\nI have completed the evaluation of grouping **EL-MD-EXP-1** (Site Feature Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines) for the **Valley View Townhomes** project.\n\n### Applicability\nThis grouping **APPLIES** to this project. Existing overhead electric utility facilities are depicted on the existing conditions survey (Sheet 4) along the western and northern property boundaries, and additional overhead electric infrastructure is shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31) along the southern boundary.\n\n### Key Research Findings\n\n**Overhead Electric Infrastructure Documented:**\n- Sheet 4 (Existing Conditions Survey): Shows multiple utility poles and overhead utility lines along western and northern boundaries, with one down guy anchor on the northern boundary\n- Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan): Shows overhead electric line (\"-OHE-\") running along the southern property boundary with 5 mitigation trees proposed directly over or adjacent to this line\n\n**Critical Documentation Gaps Identified:**\n1. **No clearance dimensions** - Overhead lines shown on site plans lack dimension annotations showing required clearances\n2. **No building elevations** - Building elevation sheets with surveyed overhead conductor elevations are not provided\n3. **Trees near overhead facilities** - The landscape plan shows 5 mitigation trees (\"M\") directly under/adjacent to the overhead line on the southern boundary\n4. **No utility compatibility verification** - The plant list (Sheet 35) specifies Cedar Elm (10) and Live Oak (2) as shade trees, which are NOT utility-compatible species (mature heights exceed 20 feet) for planting within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors\n5. **No 48-hour notification note** - Required contractor notification to Austin Energy is not documented on the plans\n6. **Guy anchor easement missing** - Down guy anchor shown but no easement documentation provided\n\n### Output Summary\n\n**Findings Submitted:**\n- **1 FAIL item**: EL-1.37 (Trees not utility-compatible for overhead line proximity)\n- **8 NOT-VERIFIABLE items**: EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.7, EL-1.8, EL-1.23, EL-1.31, EL-1.42, EL-1.46\n- **38 items not flagged**: Either not applicable to this project or no evidence of deficiency\n\nThe complete findings have been submitted in the required structured format and are ready for applicant response."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3287,"cache_read_input_tokens":65775,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3287},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1a6fb5a-883d-4cd9-82be-985a56b0d602","uuid":"61cdf160-730d-462e-8c21-eb429e7d6817"}
{"level":30,"time":1776460355553,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":2402405,"duration_api_ms":136818,"num_turns":25,"result":"## Summary of Site Plan Review Findings\n\nI have completed the evaluation of grouping **EL-MD-EXP-1** (Site Feature Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines) for the **Valley View Townhomes** project.\n\n### Applicability\nThis grouping **APPLIES** to this project. Existing overhead electric utility facilities are depicted on the existing conditions survey (Sheet 4) along the western and northern property boundaries, and additional overhead electric infrastructure is shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31) along the southern boundary.\n\n### Key Research Findings\n\n**Overhead Electric Infrastructure Documented:**\n- Sheet 4 (Existing Conditions Survey): Shows multiple utility poles and overhead utility lines along western and northern boundaries, with one down guy anchor on the northern boundary\n- Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan): Shows overhead electric line (\"-OHE-\") running along the southern property boundary with 5 mitigation trees proposed directly over or adjacent to this line\n\n**Critical Documentation Gaps Identified:**\n1. **No clearance dimensions** - Overhead lines shown on site plans lack dimension annotations showing required clearances\n2. **No building elevations** - Building elevation sheets with surveyed overhead conductor elevations are not provided\n3. **Trees near overhead facilities** - The landscape plan shows 5 mitigation trees (\"M\") directly under/adjacent to the overhead line on the southern boundary\n4. **No utility compatibility verification** - The plant list (Sheet 35) specifies Cedar Elm (10) and Live Oak (2) as shade trees, which are NOT utility-compatible species (mature heights exceed 20 feet) for planting within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors\n5. **No 48-hour notification note** - Required contractor notification to Austin Energy is not documented on the plans\n6. **Guy anchor easement missing** - Down guy anchor shown but no easement documentation provided\n\n### Output Summary\n\n**Findings Submitted:**\n- **1 FAIL item**: EL-1.37 (Trees not utility-compatible for overhead line proximity)\n- **8 NOT-VERIFIABLE items**: EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.7, EL-1.8, EL-1.23, EL-1.31, EL-1.42, EL-1.46\n- **38 items not flagged**: Either not applicable to this project or no evidence of deficiency\n\nThe complete findings have been submitted in the required structured format and are ready for applicant response.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"f1a6fb5a-883d-4cd9-82be-985a56b0d602","total_cost_usd":0.2404367,"usage":{"input_tokens":137,"cache_creation_input_tokens":70490,"cache_read_input_tokens":953122,"output_tokens":11375,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":70490,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":137,"outputTokens":11375,"cacheReadInputTokens":953122,"cacheCreationInputTokens":70490,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2404367,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"grouping":"1","findings":[{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.1","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["Proposed site features throughout site"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Overhead electric line depicted on landscape plan (Sheet 31) along southern boundary and poles shown on existing conditions survey (Sheet 4) along western and northern boundaries, but no clearance dimension annotations are shown on site plan indicating horizontal sky-to-ground clearances from outside conductors to proposed buildings and structures."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.3","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["Proposed buildings and any building projections near overhead facilities"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Building elevations and section views showing projections and their clearances from overhead electric conductors are not provided. Without elevation views with surveyed conductor locations and dimension lines to building overhangs, awnings, and balconies, clearance compliance cannot be verified."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.7","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["Buildings and elevated structures near overhead facilities"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Building elevation sheets with surveyed overhead conductor elevations and clearance dimension annotations are not included in the site plan set. Registered surveyor field measurements of overhead line heights and clearances to building features are not provided."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.8","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["Proposed buildings and site features throughout site"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Section and enlarged plan views with dimension lines showing clearances from outer electric conductor to outermost edges of buildings and projections are not provided on the site plan sheets. Clearance verification requires specific dimension annotations originating from the conductor location, not the pole."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.23","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["Overhead electric facilities throughout site"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":4}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Clearance dimensions are not labeled on site plans. Where clearance dimensions are required, they must originate from the outside conductor (outer electric wire), not from utility pole centerline or pole structure. Multi-conductor installations may have conductors several feet offset from pole center, making pole-measured clearances inaccurate."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.31","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["Overhead electric line crossing on southern boundary near proposed landscaping and trees"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":4}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Existing overhead facilities may not meet minimum vertical clearance requirements in areas with proposed landscaping. Registered surveyor mid-span clearance data is not provided to verify that overhead lines maintain adequate clearance to future grade elevations, especially where trees are proposed directly under the line."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.37","status":"fail","codeCitations":["ECM § 2.4.1.D","ECM § 2.4.2.C","UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Landscape area along southern boundary under overhead electric line"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows five mitigation trees (designated 'M') proposed directly over or immediately adjacent to the overhead electric line on the southern boundary. Plant list (Sheet 35) specifies Cedar Elm (10 trees, mature height significantly exceeds 20 feet) and Live Oak (2 trees, mature height significantly exceeds 20 feet) as proposed shade trees. These large canopy species are NOT utility-compatible for planting within 10 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors per UCM § 1.10.10.4. Only utility-compatible trees with mature height ≤20 feet (such as Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud per ECM Appendix F) are permitted within the 10-foot clearance zone. No 'UC' (Utility Compatible) designation provided for any trees."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.42","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Down guy anchor point on northern property boundary"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":4}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Existing conditions survey (Sheet 4) depicts a down guy anchor extending from the utility pole on the northern property boundary. No easement exhibit or easement documentation is provided showing a dedicated 10-foot-wide easement extending 5 feet past where the down guy enters the ground, as required for down guy anchor points."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.46","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.2"],"applicableAreas":["Entire site where overhead electric facilities exist"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":3}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No plan note is visible requiring that owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines as required by Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003. This notification requirement must appear on cover sheet or general notes with specific reference to Austin Energy."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"f39d0a4a-022b-490e-a306-64cf851fa499"}
{"level":30,"time":1776460355553,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_018Fk6BzVYnksAtmNf3HcfVq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"CRITICAL FINDINGS from the general notes:\n\nBlock 14 (Austin Energy Notes - Distribution):\n- \"Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees...\"\n- \"The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electrical facilities...\"\n- \"The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, OSHA regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. **Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained.**\"\n\nBlock 20 (Electric Transmission Notes):\n- \"No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure.\"\n- \"Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures\"\n\nThis is very important! The general notes state that:\n1. The owner is responsible for clearances\n2. Austin Energy will NOT render service unless required clearances are maintained\n3. There are specific rules about trees near transmission facilities\n\nNow, the question is: Are there transmission facilities on this site? From the vision analysis of Sheet 21, I saw \"Overhead Electric Lines (OHE)\" along the boundaries, but I need to determine if these are distribution or transmission.\n\nFrom the facts.md, I saw:\n- \"5' Electric Easements (×2)\" - these are plat-dedicated, likely distribution easements\n- No mention of transmission easement in the critical easement section\n\nFrom the grouping file, the standard Austin voltages for distribution are:\n- Distribution: < 60kV (typically 12.47kV, 34.5kV)\n- Transmission: ≥ 60kV (69kV, 138kV, 345kV)\n\n\nThe plan notes reference \"overhead electric distribution line\" in the visual analysis, suggesting these are distribution, not transmission. But the core issue remains: trees are positioned at 0 feet lateral distance from the overhead electric line, which violates clearance requirements under either classification. Additionally, the plans lack documented Austin Energy written approval for this tree placement, which is required under UCM § 1.10.10.4 and EL-2.15.\n\nBased on my analysis, I've identified clear violations. Trees measured at 0 feet and 17.5 feet from the OHE line fail EL-2.1 (non-utility-compatible trees within 15 feet of distribution conductor) or EL-2.3 (large trees within 25 feet), with medium confidence showing at least 2 trees at 0 feet from the southern line. A tree at 4 feet from pad-mounted electric equipment violates EL-2.7. The fundamental problem is that trees at 0 feet cannot satisfy any proper clearance standard, and the required Austin Energy approval documentation is absent.\n\nThe landscape plan also lacks adequate species identification directly on Sheet 31—trees are marked with generic symbols (M, SY, PL, P) while species details appear only on Sheet 35, a separate calculations sheet. This violates EL-2.10 since UCM § 1.10.10.4 requires species identification on the landscape plan itself for trees near electric facilities.\n\nAdditionally, the plans fail to differentiate large trees (40+ feet) from smaller specimens, which creates ambiguity about whether certain trees trigger the 25-foot setback requirement under EL-2.3.\n\n1. Trees positioned at 0 feet and 17.5 feet from the OHE line fall within prohibited clearance zones, yet no Austin Energy written approval documentation exists to justify these deviations. Without confirmed species identification across all boundary areas, I cannot definitively verify whether large trees are present or properly differentiated, making several violations difficult to confirm as definitive failures.\n\n2. Distribution-only facilities mean transmission-related standards don't apply. The west boundary measurements of 72.9 and 96.3 feet exceed the 5-foot minimum requirement. However, I'd need specific pole locations to assess whether southern boundary trees violate clearance standards relative to those poles. Underground equipment proximity and root barrier documentation also remain unverifiable without additional site details, and the absence of clearance dimensions on the plans prevents full evaluation of dimensional compliance.\n\nThe landscape plan does show tree locations relative to overhead electric infrastructure. Looking at what violations I can actually defend: the transformer pad violation at 4.3 feet is measurable and clear, and the failure to identify plant species for trees near electric facilities is documented on the plan itself. For the trees positioned at 0 and 17.5 feet from the overhead line, I'd need to confirm whether those species are utility-incompatible, whether they'll exceed 40 feet at maturity, or whether Austin Energy approval exists—each of these represents a potential violation path depending on what the species identification reveals.\n\nThe vision analysis suggested different symbols on the south boundary than standard plantings, possibly indicating Texas Mountain Laurel and Live Oak, but without explicit species labeling on the plan, I can't definitively establish which violations apply. If Live Oak is indeed planted at 0 feet from the line, that would clearly violate the 25-foot setback for large trees. The most defensible violations to report are the transformer pad clearance and the missing species identification, with the overhead line violations contingent on species confirmation. approval is required but doesn't confirm it was obtained. The 0-foot placement without documented approval suggests a potential EL-2.15 violation.\n\nMy assessment: EL-2.10 is clearly a FAIL since species aren't identified on the plan. EL-2.7 fails due to the 4.3-foot distance from the transformer. For EL-2.15, the absence of documented Austin Energy approval for trees positioned at 0 feet from the overhead line indicates non-compliance. EL-2.1, EL-2.3, and EL-2.11 remain NOT-VERIFIABLE without species identification, which is the root deficiency flagged under EL-2.10. For EL-2.15, I need to verify three conditions: whether trees occupy prohibited clearance zones (they do—at 0, 0, and 17.5 feet from the overhead electric line), whether prohibited zones exist per code (they do—less than 15 or 25 feet depending on species), and whether Austin Energy approval documentation is present (it isn't). This deficiency should be marked as FAIL since all three conditions are met and no approval letter has been located.\n\nHowever, the critical issue is determining what actually constitutes a \"prohibited\" zone. The clearance requirements are conditional—trees under 15 feet laterally can only be utility-compatible species, while the 15-25 foot band allows either utility-compatible trees or smaller non-utility-compatible ones. Without confirming the species and mature heights of the trees at 0 and 17.5 feet, I can't definitively classify these as violations versus restricted areas needing approval. That said, the 0-foot placement (directly beneath the line) combined with the absence of any documented approval strongly suggests this should be flagged.\n\nI'll categorize the findings as:\n\n**FAIL:** EL-2.7 (tree at 4.3 feet from transformer, below the 10-foot minimum) and EL-2.10 (species not identified on the landscape plan itself, only on a separate sheet).\n\n**NOT-VERIFIABLE:** EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.11, and EL-2.15 all hinge on species identification and utility-compatibility status. The trees positioned at 0, 0, and 17.5 feet from the overhead line cannot be properly evaluated without confirming their botanical characteristics and whether Austin Energy approval exists for any restricted-zone plantings.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2274,"cache_read_input_tokens":73127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2274},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e54b4ec-2280-4055-b825-43898abbf7bc","uuid":"2a64a535-985f-4a1e-bbbe-942d865196ca"}
{"level":30,"time":1776460355553,"pid":85817,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_018Fk6BzVYnksAtmNf3HcfVq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my comprehensive research, measurements, and document review, I can now compile my findings. I've identified critical violations related to tree clearances from electric equipment and inadequate species identification on the landscape plan.\n\n## Summary of Research Findings:\n\n**Electric Infrastructure Identified:**\n- Overhead electric distribution lines (OHE) along western and southern property boundaries\n- Underground electric distribution lines forming looped network through site\n- Multiple transformer pads (5 total) at various locations\n- Electric pull boxes at 7 locations\n- Electrical meters at buildings\n\n**Tree Placement Issues Identified:**\n1. **Southern boundary trees near OHE line**: Measurements show trees at 0 feet (2 trees), 0 feet, and 17.5 feet lateral distance from overhead electric line - suggesting inappropriate proximity\n2. **Transformer pad west of Bldg. 1**: Nearest tree at 4.3 feet lateral distance (violates 10-foot minimum)\n3. **Species identification deficiency**: Trees shown on Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) with symbols only (M, SY, PL, P); species identification only appears on Sheet 35, a separate calculations sheet\n\n**Critical Documentation Gap:**\n- Sheet 31 contains NO plant species legend or identification for trees shown near electric facilities\n- Per UCM § 1.10.10.4, species identification is necessary to evaluate utility-compatibility status and mature height thresholds\n- No evidence of documented Austin Energy written approval for trees positioned at/near overhead lines"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2274,"cache_read_inp